{% extends 'layout/shell.twig' %}

{#
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  NOMINATION SENT — THE MOMENT, AND THE THREE THINGS IT OWES
  docs/redesign-ref/Nominate Page.dc.html (the DONE state) · app-ux §24
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  This page was the last of the three nomination screens still on the legacy layout,
  with forty lines of inline `style`, a Lottie trophy, and `sc_confetti: false` — so
  the one screen in the flow that §24 makes a MUST was the one with nothing on it.

  ── WHY A NOMINATION CELEBRATES AT ALL, AND WHY NOT LIKE A WIN ──────────────

  §24's basis is the peak–end rule: what somebody remembers of a flow is its peak and
  its end, and this is both. Somebody has just spent ninety seconds writing about a
  person they admire, for no return to themselves.

  But a nomination is NOT a win, and this platform decides awards. Firing the winner's
  choreography here would tell a nominator the award is theirs, on the screen where
  that is most likely to be believed. `celebrate.js` carries a `nominate` kind for
  exactly that reason — stars from the badge, half the density, no gold cannons —
  and `data-celebrate-kind` is how this page asks for it.

  `data-celebrate` keys on the REFERENCE, so somebody who nominates three people sees
  three moments and somebody who reopens this page sees a document. That is §24.4's
  once-per-achievement rule, and the engine has always worked that way.

  ── IT DOES NOT PAINT THE PAGE ──────────────────────────────────────────────

  The name, the reference and every sentence here are the server's output and are
  complete before a byte of JavaScript arrives. If the script never loads, this is a
  page with no stars — never a page with no confirmation. This codebase has shipped a
  dead camera, a mute door and a stop-link nobody was handed; a success page must not
  join them.
#}

{% set _nom = nominee|default('') %}
{% set _cat = category|default('') %}
{% set _ref = ref|default('') %}

{% block head_styles %}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('/assets/css/components/nominate.css') }}">
{% endblock %}

{% block header %}{% include 'partials/site-header.twig' %}{% endblock %}

{#
  THE CHILD VARIANT, BECAUSE THE PAGE'S OWN MESSAGE IS THE HEADING. The root variant
  renders its large title as the page's `<h1>` — correct on a tab destination, and on
  this page it meant TWO visible `<h1>`s on a phone, "Nominate" above "Your nomination
  is in." §16 allows one. The child bar has no `<h1>` of its own, so the sentence a
  person came here to read is the heading, which is also what it is.

  Back goes to the hub rather than to the form just submitted: returning somebody to a
  completed form is how a nomination gets sent twice.
#}
{% block app_bar %}
  {% include 'partials/app-bar.twig' with {
    variant: 'child', back: '/nominate', title: 'Nomination sent'
  } %}
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<div class="nf nf--done">

  <div class="ns" data-celebrate="{{ _ref ?: 'nomination' }}" data-celebrate-kind="nominate">

    <span class="ns__badge" data-celebrate-anchor aria-hidden="true">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
           stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
        <path d="m12 3 2.6 6.1 6.4.5-4.9 4.2 1.5 6.2L12 16.8 6.4 20l1.5-6.2L3 9.6l6.4-.5Z"/>
      </svg>
    </span>

    <p class="ns__kicker">Nomination sent</p>
    <h1 class="ns__h">
      {% if _nom %}{{ _nom }} has been put forward.{% else %}Your nomination is in.{% endif %}
    </h1>

    {# The sentence carries MEANING rather than praise — §24.4. What happens next is
       the thing a nominator actually wants to know, and it is the platform's promise
       rather than a compliment about them. #}
    <p class="ns__sub">
      {% if _cat %}Filed under {{ _cat }}. {% endif %}Our panel reviews every nomination
      before a profile joins the cycle{% if review_sla_hours %}, usually within
      {{ review_sla_hours }} hours{% endif %} — and we email you the moment it is live.
    </p>

    {% if _ref %}
      <dl class="ns__ref">
        <dt>Reference</dt>
        <dd>{{ _ref }}</dd>
      </dl>
    {% endif %}

    {# §24.4: the primary is THE NEXT BEST THING, not "go home". For a nominator that
       is telling other people, because a nomination with more voices behind it is a
       stronger case — which is what the share block below actually does. #}
    <div class="ns__do">
      {% if share_payload %}
        <a class="ag-btn ag-btn--primary ag-btn--block" href="#ns-share">Rally more nominations</a>
      {% else %}
        <a class="ag-btn ag-btn--primary ag-btn--block" href="/nominate">Nominate someone else</a>
      {% endif %}
      <a class="ag-btn ag-btn--secondary ag-btn--block" href="/leaderboard">See the leaderboard</a>
    </div>

    <p class="ns__fine">
      We have also told {{ _nom ?: 'the nominee' }} on the contact you shared.
      <a href="/integrity">How a winner is decided</a> ·
      <a href="/philosophy">Why votes are not the whole story</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  {% if share_payload %}
    {#
      A SHARED LINK PREFILLS WHAT THIS NOMINATOR ALREADY KNEW, so the next person adds
      their own voice rather than retyping a stranger's details. It is the same
      mechanism `/nominate/{slug}?share=` reads, and the expiry is stated because a
      link that silently stops working reads as the site being broken.
    #}
    <section class="ns__share ag-card" id="ns-share" data-ag-share>
      {#
        A `<template>`, and neither a `data-` attribute nor a script block.

        · Not an attribute, because a nominee's name can carry an apostrophe and the
          parser closes an attribute at the first one.
        · Not `<script type="application/json">`, because `CspTest` sweeps every
          template for an un-nonced inline script and is right to: the distinction
          between an executable type and a data one is one typo wide, and a sweep
          that has to know the difference is a sweep with an exception in it.

        A `<template>` is parsed and not rendered, holds no script, and raises no
        policy question at all. `json_encode(15)` escapes the angle brackets, so the
        payload cannot close it early.
      #}
      <template data-ag-share-payload>{{ share_payload|json_encode(15)|raw }}</template>
      <h2>Rally more nominations for {{ _nom ?: 'them' }}</h2>
      <p>Nominations from more people and places strengthen a case. Share a link that
        fills in what you knew — whoever opens it can change every detail before
        submitting in their own name.</p>

      <button type="button" class="ag-btn ag-btn--primary" data-ag-share-make>
        Get a share link
      </button>

      <p class="ns__share-err" role="alert" data-ag-share-err hidden></p>

      <div class="ns__share-out" data-ag-share-out hidden>
        <label class="ag-label" for="ns-share-url">Your share link</label>
        <div class="ns__share-row">
          <input id="ns-share-url" class="ag-field" type="text" readonly data-ag-share-url>
          <button type="button" class="ag-btn ag-btn--ink" data-ag-share-copy>Copy</button>
        </div>
        <p class="ag-hint" data-ag-share-life></p>
      </div>
    </section>
  {% endif %}
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block foot_scripts %}
  {# Both deferred, and neither renders anything. See the header note: the page is the
     page without them. #}
  <script defer src="{{ asset('/assets/js/vendor/canvas-confetti-1.9.3.js') }}"></script>
  <script defer src="{{ asset('/assets/js/celebrate.js') }}"></script>
  {% if share_payload %}
    <script defer src="{{ asset('/assets/js/nominate-share.js') }}"></script>
  {% endif %}
{% endblock %}
